// ═══ {{ feature }} ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// One component's tests, sealed behind its Cargo feature. The `#[cfg]` — not the module — is what
// isolates them: an inherent `impl Fixture` contributes its methods to the type GLOBALLY (rustc
// E0592 fires on a duplicate method name even when the two `impl` blocks sit in different modules),
// so a module alone would not stop two components' helpers from colliding. Gating means only ONE
// section's items ever exist in a build, and since each `#[invariant_test]` expands to its own
// `fn main()` + `#[global_allocator]`, exactly one feature is ever enabled. Components can
// therefore define same-named helpers forever without interfering.
//
// The module is the single item the `#[cfg]` hangs on, which is why the section is not gated
// item-by-item: doing that would mean parsing item boundaries out of authored source. A
// `#[cfg]`-disabled `mod` is stripped before rustc resolves its file, so a build selecting one
// feature needs only that component's file on disk.
#[cfg(feature = "{{ feature }}")]
mod {{ feature }};

// The entry point is OURS, generated: `#[invariant_test]` expands `fn main()` as a SIBLING of the
// fn it annotates, so it has to be applied at crate root — the authored fn cannot carry it from
// inside the module. The macro INLINES the annotated body into its per-action loop, so delegating
// from here runs the authored fn once after each fuzzed action, which is the semantics we want. We
// do not reimplement `main` — that is ~100 lines of libafl wiring we would then own across
// Crucible bumps.
#[cfg(feature = "{{ feature }}")]
#[invariant_test]
fn {{ feature }}(fixture: &mut Fixture) {
    {{ feature }}::{{ section_fn }}(fixture)
}
